            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new run of words: what did pal mean, and what did vayiṟu mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ஆசிரியர்",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ஆசிரியர் (āsiriyar) — \"a teacher\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit ācārya, one who teaches by how they behave, arriving in Tamil with -ar on the end — the respectful ending already met on ivar and naṇbar. So the word is polite before it is anything else: there is no way to say it that does not show regard."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tamil keeps the older and plainer ஆசான் (āsāṉ) beside it, and that one is what a student of an art calls a master."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of five ways to say who somebody is."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
