          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "விவசாயி (vivasāyi) — \"a farmer\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ending is -i, which Tamil hangs on somebody by their work rather than by their sex, so the one word covers a man and a woman alike."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The stem is borrowed. The inherited word is உழவன் (uḻavaṉ), built straight on uḻu, 'to plough', and it carries an older weight — Tamil poetry praises the uḻavaṉ, while a form at an office asks for the vivasāyi. Two words, one occupation, two registers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four, and the fourth one feeds the other three."
            }
          ]
        },
